Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

WINDOWS X64 TARGET: RELEASE 0.9.0

The final merged compiler includes original Windows mode7 (--windows),
Zero OS app modes8/9 and the real-mode biosmove primitive. The Windows target
emits native console PE64 files with KERNEL32 binary stdin/stdout and exit,
an original bounded argc/argv parser, a 4 MiB Zero arena and 16 MiB committed
stack. Product implementation is original Zero and original emitted x64 code.
No Linux, CRT, foreign-language runtime, external compiler or assembler is
required by the Windows distribution.

Final live compiler source SHA256:
01ee47113ab98da6ea4d84c042e570d4080a7ab69473acc205923bd6ba7839fc
Final Linux bootstrap/build compiler SHA256:
7a24c5e3d23bbeb6df6b25a30f99efe26e82aa9f9c835d4aba45000a61d75cb6

Verification host: Debian GNU/Linux 13 x86-64. External Wine 10.0, Debian
10.0~repack-6, extracted outside the product under /workspace/wine-local.
Wine server data paths were relocated to that extracted directory and Wine's
official libz-mingw-w64 dependency supplied for Wine's own UI DLL loading.
No Wine/package code is included in the Windows distribution.
No native Microsoft Windows machine run has been performed.

PASS: 25/25 ABI and PE tests, including byte-identical native Windows compiler
self-compilation under Wine; byte-identical Linux/raw/raw32/EFI/EFI32/real/
app64/app32 cross-target output; a 512 KiB binary pipe round-trip; EOF/count
limits/statuses; broken-pipe put exits74; quoted executable paths; explicit
hardware/BIOSmove/Unix primitive rejection; existing cooperative contexts
with128 rounds per worker; no writable/executable PE section; KERNEL32 only.
PASS: 85/85 Ezero behavioral cases using the Windows PE frontend, packer,
backend and generated executable under Wine. Covers UTF-8 phrases, one-based
collections, functions, recursion, automatic collection, errors and capacities.
PASS: 12/12 Windows wrapper/checker cases, including paths with spaces,
ezero-run, source/output alias rejection, preserving existing output on
syntax error, complete rebuild.cmd self-reproduction and negative cases for
the included original Zero binary comparison helper.

Evidence outside build:
  verification/windows-0.9.0/SUMMARY.ztext
  verification/windows-0.9.0/manifest.json
  verification/windows-0.9.0/native-verification/results.json
  verification/windows-0.9.0/verification/results.json
  verification/windows-0.9.0/package-check/wrapper-results.json

Clean distributable:
  build/zero-ezero-windows-x64/
  build/zero-ezero-windows-x64.zip

Source integration parts are retained for review:
  x64-windows.zero.part overrides70 and41..45 only in mode7, with no new IDs.
  finalize-windows.zero.part emits PE64/import/section structures in Zero.
  apply.py is host-only source integration, not a language implementation.
  compiler-windows.zero retains the earlier standalone integration candidate;
  the release builds from the final root compiler.zero, not this candidate.

portability/windows/build.sh packages from merged build/zero/compiler.zero.
Product wrappers use native Windows .cmd, and rebuild all executables using
Zero. Python/shell verification scripts only orchestrate tests/packaging.
